.TH ENTRAINE 1 "2026-08-01" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
entraine \- train the hands on keystrokes that survive a fresh machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B entraine
.br
.B entraine play
[\fB\-\-level\fR \fIn\fR]
.br
.B entraine levels
.br
.B entraine tips
[\fIlevel\fR]
.br
.B entraine progress
.br
.B entraine reset
.br
.B entraine panes
.br
.B entraine git
.br
.B entraine simulate
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B entraine
teaches vim by making the keystroke the game mechanic rather than the
subject of a lesson. The learner is a cursor on an ASCII grid; movement is
real vim motions, unlocked one level at a time, and a level is winnable
only with the vocabulary already taught.
.PP
The bar it is built against is competence that survives a fresh machine:
stock vim, no
.IR ~/.vimrc ,
no plugins. A trick that needs a dotfile is not the skill.
.PP
.B THIS PAGE IS AHEAD OF THE TOOL, ON PURPOSE.
The teaching logic underneath it is real, tested by name, and free to run
\- but none of it is reachable as a verb, and until it is, every
subcommand below is a promise this utility owes rather than a behaviour it
has. The page is written first so the implementation has something to be
judged against; see
.I GAPS.md
for what is owed, and HISTORY for why it is written this way round.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B play
Start, or resume at the level last reached. Requires a terminal to render
into; with no TTY this is 6 (BLIND), not a crash and not silence.
.TP
.BI "play \-\-level" " n"
Start at a specific level instead of the one last reached. Progress is not
rewritten by this.
.TP
.B levels
The level table: each level, the motion or concept it introduces, and the
cumulative vocabulary unlocked by the end of it. Needs no terminal.
.TP
.BI tips " level"
The tip text a learner is shown before a level, printed as text. With no
argument, the introductory tips \- which include the three safe routes for
pasting INTO vim without autoindent staircasing it. Needs no terminal.
.TP
.B progress
The level last reached. A missing save file is "start over" and exits 0; a
save file that exists and cannot be read is 6 (BLIND), because those are
two different world\-states.
.TP
.B reset
Discard saved progress, so the next play starts fresh.
.TP
.B panes
Teach tmux pane splitting and focus movement. The mechanic exists and is
tested; no learner can reach it, so this is 4 (GAP) and names what is
missing rather than pretending.
.TP
.B git
Teach git hygiene \- why a commit message, small commits, fetch before you
start, resolving a real conflict. Designed and not built: the shape is
decided, the delivery is not, so this is 4 (GAP).
.TP
.B simulate
Print what a command would have said, instead of making the learner run
it. Refused, always, with 7. A tutor that simulates output teaches trust
in the tutor rather than in the tool.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-level " n"
.B play
only. The level to start at.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output, for
.B levels
and
.BR progress .
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Usage summary.
.SH COST
.B entraine
cannot spend money. It carries no
.B \-\-summon
flag, and no subcommand of it reaches a metered service.
.PP
This is not merely "not implemented yet". A summon is available on a gap
and forbidden on a refusal: asking this utility to teach git as an
ASCII\-grid game, or to simulate command output, must exit 7 and spend
nothing, because having no escalation path is what refusing on principle
means.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. An unknown subcommand is this.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: in scope, not yet mechanized. The message names its own escalation.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran and produced a wrong or partial answer.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read its domain \- no terminal to render into, or a
progress file that exists and cannot be read. Never reported as "nothing
to report".
.TP
.B 7
REFUSED: out of scope on principle, listed under REFUSALS. No
.B \-\-summon
will change it.
.SH REFUSALS
Quoted from the project rather than reasoned out here, because a refusal
that drifts is not a principle:
.TP
.B teaching anything that needs a dotfile
Stock vim only. The whole point is competence that survives a fresh
machine, and a paste trick that needs a
.I ~/.vimrc
is not the skill.
.TP
.B simulating command output
Make the learner run the real command and report the real output back.
.TP
.B teaching git as an ASCII-grid game
vim moves a cursor over static text; git operates on live stateful
history, and the honest teaching moment does not survive the metaphor
without faking the state underneath it.
.TP
.B quoting vimtutor's text
Vim\-licensed. The tips are written fresh, and a test enforces it.
.TP
.B driving a real tmux subprocess
The pane mechanic is a grid\-world metaphor, deliberately, to stay
curses\-free and unit\-testable \- at the stated cost of teaching
keybinding muscle memory only.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask what a level teaches before playing it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ entraine tips 6
.fi
.RE
.PP
Resume where the last session stopped:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ entraine play
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask an unattended caller's question \- what level was reached \- without
needing a terminal at all:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ entraine progress \-\-json
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B None of the examples above is a doctest yet,
and the page says so rather than showing invented output. An example with
output that was never executed is a lie with a shell prompt in front of
it, so the outputs are withheld until the subcommands exist.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I CONTRACT.md
The promise this utility must keep, obligation by obligation.
.TP
.I GAPS.md
What the contract names and the tooling cannot yet do.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ENTRAINE_LEGACY_ROOT
The checkout holding the teaching logic this verb fronts. Defaults to the
.B vim-arcade
working tree.
.SH HISTORY
Written 2026\-08\-01, and written BEFORE the tool, which is the whole
method: a verb coined with a definition exists, and speaking it forces the
implementation to become true to the definition.
.PP
The previous page described a utility in general terms and listed no
subcommands, because there were none to discover. Its own contract named
the missing thing exactly \- an argv surface, "settled by deciding what a
nightly caller would ask
.B entraine
for, which nothing yet does". This page is that decision.
.PP
This branch is a total purge: it keeps the tool and nothing else. What was
removed is on the default branch of this same repository, which is why
this is a branch and not a new repository.
